<p>In 1986, Michael Kinsley, editor of <em>The New Republic</em>, spotted the headline <a href="https://www.nytimes.com/1986/04/10/opinion/foreign-affairs-worthwhile-canadian-initiative.html">&#8220;Worthwhile Canadian Initiative&#8221;</a> in the <em>New York Times</em> and <a href="https://newrepublic.com/article/127231/worthwhile-canadian-initiative">challenged his readers</a> to come up with a more boring one. This was a tall order: The <em>Times</em> head, someone said, manages to be &#8220;not only boring as a whole, but boring word by word.&#8221; Submissions included:</p>
<ul>
<li>&#8220;U.S. Leadership Needed&#8221; (<em>New York Times</em>)</li>
<li>&#8220;Trade, A Two-Way Street&#8221; (<em>New York Times</em>)</li>
<li>&#8220;Beyond the News, Larger Issues&#8221; (<em>New York Times</em>)</li>
<li>&#8220;Thoughts at Graduation Time&#8221; (<em>New York Times</em>)</li>
<li>&#8220;Continue Manned Flight in Space&#8221; (<em>New York Times</em>)</li>
<li>&#8220;Chill Falls on Warming Relations Between Australia and Indonesia&#8221; (<em>New York Times</em>)</li>
<li>&#8220;University of Rochester Decides to Keep Name&#8221; (<em>New York Times</em>)</li>
<li>&#8220;Dramatic Changes Fail to Materialize on Hill&#8221; (<em>Washington Post</em>)</li>
<li>&#8220;Surprises Unlikely in Indiana&#8221; (<em>Chicago Tribune</em>)</li>
<li>&#8220;CIA Analysis Sees Soviet Economy in Need of Changes&#8221; (<em>San Diego Union</em>)</li>
<li>&#8220;Prevent Burglary by Locking House, Detectives Urge&#8221; (<em>Boston Globe</em>)</li>
<li>&#8220;Methodists Oppose Use of Nuclear Arms&#8221; (<em>New York Times</em>)</li>
<li>&#8220;Economist Dies&#8221; (<em>Wisconsin State Journal</em>)</li>
<li>&#8220;Turbulent Days for Donald D. Engen&#8221; (<em>New York Times</em>)</li>
</ul>
<p><a href="https://newrepublic.com/article/83792/dont-stop-the-presses">The winner</a> was found in the <em>New York Times</em> science section: &#8220;Debate Goes on Over the Nature of Reality.&#8221;</p>
